<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:controls="using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Controls">

    <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>
        <!-- NOTE: When the per-control themeresources files are merged into the master
             themeresources.xaml, other controls (e.g. NumberBox) contribute "Light" theme
             dictionaries. Once a "Light" dict exists at the merged level, XAML treats
             "Default" as the Dark theme rather than as a fallback for Light. So if we only
             declared "Default" + "HighContrast", these resources would be missing from the
             Light theme at runtime, causing "Cannot find a Resource" failures during style
             application. We therefore explicitly declare "Light", "Default" (Dark), and
             "HighContrast" with the same values. -->
        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Light">
            <!-- TableView itself binds Background / Foreground / BorderBrush / header chrome
                 through the TabularSurface* tokens. The canonical MUX_-prefixed definitions
                 live in controls/dev/CommonStyles/TabularSurfaces_themeresources.xaml
                 (Phase 1.7 docs/design-notes/TableView/primitives/TabularSurfaces.md), but
                 those keys are MUX_-prefixed while the TableView control template + row code
                 look them up unprefixed. We therefore ship the unprefixed keys inside the
                 TableView control's own theme dictionary chain to guarantee the lookup
                 succeeds for every consumer (NuGet, framework merge, test apps) regardless
                 of whether CommonStyles is also merged. Values mirror the MUX_-prefixed
                 definitions in TabularSurfaces_themeresources.xaml so the visual identity
                 stays consistent whichever resource pipeline a consumer wires up.
                 Without the base TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundBrush key the implicit
                 TableViewRow Setter at TableView.xaml resolves to an unbound brush, leaving rows
                 transparent (and in HC, violating contrast requirements). -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundBrush" Color="#00FFFFFF" />                          <!-- SubtleFillColorTransparent -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundAlternatingBrush" Color="#09000000" />               <!-- SubtleFillColorSecondary (Light) -->
            <!-- c7-B1: row state brushes referenced by TableView.xaml VisualStates -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundPointerOverBrush" Color="#09000000" />               <!-- SubtleFillColorSecondary (Light) -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundPressedBrush" Color="#06000000" />                   <!-- SubtleFillColorTertiary (Light) -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundDisabledBrush" Color="#00FFFFFF" />                  <!-- SubtleFillColorTransparent -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundSelectedBrush" Color="#09000000" />                  <!-- SubtleFillColorSecondary (Light) -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundSelectedPointerOverBrush" Color="#06000000" />       <!-- SubtleFillColorTertiary (Light) -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundSelectedPressedBrush" Color="#09000000" />           <!-- SubtleFillColorSecondary (Light) -->
            <!-- c7-B1: row foreground + disabled state brush -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowForegroundBrush" Color="#E4000000" />                          <!-- TextFillColorPrimary (Light) -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowForegroundDisabledBrush" Color="#5C000000" />                  <!-- TextFillColorDisabled (Light) -->
            <!-- c7-B1 + POC GridLines visibility: gridline + header background referenced by TableView.xaml row+header chrome.
                 TabularSurfaceGridLineBrush: control-side fallback so GridLinesVisibility renders
                 visibly even when consumers do not merge the sample TabularSurfaces dictionary.
                 Value = DividerStrokeColorDefaultBrush canonical (~16% on both Light #29000000
                 and Dark #29FFFFFF — Dark was bumped from ~8% to ~16% during race-3 for
                 readability over row banding). Opaque enough to read above row banding without
                 dominating the surface. Earlier ControlStrokeColorDefault (~6% black / ~7% white)
                 was too faint to perceive at 1-px width on alternating rows; WPF DataGrid uses
                 Brushes.Black (100%) by default. We split the difference toward Fluent's divider
                 token. -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceGridLineBrush" Color="#29000000" />                               <!-- DividerStrokeColorDefault (Light) — POC visibility bump from c7-B1 #0F000000 -->
            <!-- TabularSurfaceHeaderBackgroundBrush: control-side fallback so the header strip
                 is visibly darker than data rows (WPF DataGrid header convention) even when
                 consumers don't merge the sample TabularSurfaces dictionary. ~15% black on
                 Light gives a clear step (~38-unit RGB delta on #F9F9F9 page background)
                 above row banding without dominating the surface. Bumped from #19 (~10%)
                 after the prior value rendered ~24-unit delta vs even row #F9F9F9 — calmly
                 differentiated header band, between AG-Grid Material and Notion. -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceHeaderBackgroundBrush" Color="#26000000" />                       <!-- ~15% black -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceSelectionIndicatorBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemAccentColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderBackgroundBrush" Color="#80FFFFFF" />                  <!-- LayerFillColorDefault (Light) -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderForegroundBrush" Color="#E4000000" />                  <!-- TextFillColorPrimary (Light) -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderCountForegroundBrush" Color="#9E000000" />             <!-- TextFillColorSecondary (Light) -->
            <x:String x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderChevronGlyphExpanded">&#xE972;</x:String>
            <x:String x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderChevronGlyphCollapsed">&#xE971;</x:String>
            <x:Double x:Key="TableViewRowMinHeight">28</x:Double>

            <!-- N2: header-cell chrome metrics. Previously inlined as magic numbers in
                 RebuildHeaders / AppendResizeGripperVisual; now exposed as theme tokens so
                 designers can tune the indicator spacing / header padding without a code edit. -->
            <Thickness x:Key="TableViewHeaderCellPadding">8,4,8,4</Thickness>
            <Thickness x:Key="TableViewHeaderIndicatorsMargin">0,0,12,0</Thickness>
            <x:Double x:Key="TableViewResizeGripperWidth">8</x:Double>

            <!-- Q2/M3: header text weight is themeable. Default Bold matches WPF DataGrid,
                 CommunityToolkit DataGrid, AG Grid, and Material/Notion/Airtable convention
                 where column titles are visually weightier than row text. Consumers can
                 override per-theme via standard ResourceDictionary merging without recompiling
                 the control. ContentPresenter forwards FontWeight to the default TextBlock it
                 instantiates when Content is an hstring (declarative Header="Name" — the
                 common case). HeaderTemplate / HeaderTemplateSelector consumers receive their
                 own template's explicit FontWeight unchanged (see TableView.cpp guard). -->
            <FontWeight x:Key="TableViewHeaderFontWeight">Bold</FontWeight>

            <!-- iter-12 Fix-N: TableView owns its marquee-adornment brushes rather
                 than borrowing the MarqueeSelector primitive's MUX_MarqueeSelection*
                 keys. The MarqueeSelector theme dict is a separate ResourceDictionary
                 — in theme-slice packaging where TableView ships without that
                 primitive's themeresources merged in, TableView.xaml's PART_MarqueeAdornment
                 silently fails brush lookup and the rubber-band selection rectangle
                 renders as an unfilled/unstroked invisible Rectangle. Values mirror
                 the MarqueeSelector_themeresources.xaml Light dictionary so the visual
                 identity is consistent when both controls ship together. -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MUX_TableViewMarqueeSelectionFillBrush" Color="#3F005FB8" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MUX_TableViewMarqueeSelectionStrokeBrush" Color="#FF005FB8" />

            <!-- Canonical "default banding" palette consumers can opt into without
                 hand-rolling their own theme-aware brushes. The four per-row DPs
                 (RowBackground / AlternatingRowBackground / RowForeground /
                 AlternatingRowForeground) default to null — banding stays opt-in —
                 but a consumer wanting Excel-style zebra simply binds to these:
                     <TableView RowBackground="{ThemeResource TableViewDefaultRowBackgroundBrush}"
                                AlternatingRowBackground="{ThemeResource TableViewDefaultAlternatingRowBackgroundBrush}"
                                RowForeground="{ThemeResource TableViewDefaultRowForegroundBrush}"
                                AlternatingRowForeground="{ThemeResource TableViewDefaultRowForegroundBrush}" />
                 Light values mirror Excel + WPF DataGrid convention (pure white +
                 240-grey + near-black text). Dark + HC values keep contrast bounded. -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewDefaultRowBackgroundBrush" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewDefaultAlternatingRowBackgroundBrush" Color="#FFF0F0F0" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewDefaultRowForegroundBrush" Color="#FF1F1F1F" />
        </ResourceDictionary>
        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Default">
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundBrush" Color="#00FFFFFF" />                          <!-- SubtleFillColorTransparent -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundAlternatingBrush" Color="#0FFFFFFF" />               <!-- SubtleFillColorSecondary -->
            <!-- c7-B1: row state brushes referenced by TableView.xaml VisualStates -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundPointerOverBrush" Color="#0FFFFFFF" />               <!-- SubtleFillColorSecondary -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundPressedBrush" Color="#0AFFFFFF" />                   <!-- SubtleFillColorTertiary -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundDisabledBrush" Color="#00FFFFFF" />                  <!-- SubtleFillColorTransparent -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundSelectedBrush" Color="#0FFFFFFF" />                  <!-- SubtleFillColorSecondary -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundSelectedPointerOverBrush" Color="#0AFFFFFF" />       <!-- SubtleFillColorTertiary -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundSelectedPressedBrush" Color="#0FFFFFFF" />           <!-- SubtleFillColorSecondary -->
            <!-- c7-B1: row foreground + disabled state brush -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowForegroundBrush" Color="#FFFFFF" />                            <!-- TextFillColorPrimary -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowForegroundDisabledBrush" Color="#5DFFFFFF" />                  <!-- TextFillColorDisabled -->
            <!-- c7-B1 + POC GridLines visibility: gridline + header background. See Light dict for full rationale. -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceGridLineBrush" Color="#29FFFFFF" />                               <!-- DividerStrokeColorDefault (Dark) — bumped 8.2%→16.1% for readability over banding -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceHeaderBackgroundBrush" Color="#38FFFFFF" />                       <!-- ~15% white — visible step above Dark row banding (bumped from #19 for proportional parity with Light) -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceSelectionIndicatorBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemAccentColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderBackgroundBrush" Color="#80525252" />                  <!-- LayerFillColorDefault -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderForegroundBrush" Color="#FFFFFF" />                    <!-- TextFillColorPrimary -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderCountForegroundBrush" Color="#C5FFFFFF" />             <!-- TextFillColorSecondary -->
            <x:String x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderChevronGlyphExpanded">&#xE972;</x:String>
            <x:String x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderChevronGlyphCollapsed">&#xE971;</x:String>
            <x:Double x:Key="TableViewRowMinHeight">28</x:Double>
            <Thickness x:Key="TableViewHeaderCellPadding">8,4,8,4</Thickness>
            <Thickness x:Key="TableViewHeaderIndicatorsMargin">0,0,12,0</Thickness>
            <x:Double x:Key="TableViewResizeGripperWidth">8</x:Double>
            <FontWeight x:Key="TableViewHeaderFontWeight">Bold</FontWeight>

            <!-- iter-12 Fix-N: TableView-owned marquee brushes (Dark theme). See Light
                 dictionary for rationale. -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MUX_TableViewMarqueeSelectionFillBrush" Color="#3F0096FF" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MUX_TableViewMarqueeSelectionStrokeBrush" Color="#FF0078D4" />

            <!-- Canonical "default banding" palette (Dark). See Light dict for rationale. -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewDefaultRowBackgroundBrush" Color="#FF202020" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewDefaultAlternatingRowBackgroundBrush" Color="#FF2D2D2D" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewDefaultRowForegroundBrush" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
        </ResourceDictionary>
        <ResourceDictionary x:Key="HighContrast">
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorWindowColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundAlternatingBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorWindowColor}" />
            <!-- c7-B1: row state brushes referenced by TableView.xaml VisualStates -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundPointerOverBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorHighlightColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundPressedBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorHighlightColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundDisabledBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorWindowColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundSelectedBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorHighlightColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundSelectedPointerOverBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorHighlightColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowBackgroundSelectedPressedBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorHighlightColor}" />
            <!-- c7-B1: row foreground + disabled state brush -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowForegroundBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorWindowTextColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceRowForegroundDisabledBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorGrayTextColor}" />
            <!-- c7-B1 + POC: gridline + header background referenced by TableView.xaml row+header chrome -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceGridLineBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorWindowTextColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceHeaderBackgroundBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorWindowColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceSelectionIndicatorBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorHighlightColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderBackgroundBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorWindowColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderForegroundBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorWindowTextColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderCountForegroundBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorGrayTextColor}" />
            <x:String x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderChevronGlyphExpanded">&#xE972;</x:String>
            <x:String x:Key="TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderChevronGlyphCollapsed">&#xE971;</x:String>
            <x:Double x:Key="TableViewRowMinHeight">28</x:Double>
            <Thickness x:Key="TableViewHeaderCellPadding">8,4,8,4</Thickness>
            <Thickness x:Key="TableViewHeaderIndicatorsMargin">0,0,12,0</Thickness>
            <x:Double x:Key="TableViewResizeGripperWidth">8</x:Double>
            <FontWeight x:Key="TableViewHeaderFontWeight">Bold</FontWeight>

            <!-- iter-12 Fix-N: TableView-owned marquee brushes (HighContrast). Use
                 ThemeResource SystemColorHighlightColor to track the HC scheme. The
                 fill brush carries a local Opacity="0.3" (applied directly on the
                 SolidColorBrush below) to soften the system highlight, matching
                 MarqueeSelector_themeresources HighContrast which uses the same
                 {ThemeResource SystemColorHighlightColor} with Opacity 0.3 on the
                 brush element. The 1px Stroke is the dominant visual cue in HC;
                 identical to sibling primitive behavior. -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MUX_TableViewMarqueeSelectionFillBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorHighlightColor}" Opacity="0.3" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="MUX_TableViewMarqueeSelectionStrokeBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorHighlightColor}" />

            <!-- Canonical "default banding" palette (HighContrast). Both background brushes
                 use SystemColorWindowColor (no banding distinction in HC — required by HC
                 contrast contracts) and foreground tracks SystemColorWindowTextColor. -->
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewDefaultRowBackgroundBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorWindowColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewDefaultAlternatingRowBackgroundBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorWindowColor}" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TableViewDefaultRowForegroundBrush" Color="{ThemeResource SystemColorWindowTextColor}" />
        </ResourceDictionary>
    </ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

    <x:Double x:Key="TableViewSelectionGutterWidth">32</x:Double>

    <Style x:Key="TableViewSelectionGutterCheckBoxStyle"
           TargetType="CheckBox"
           BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultCheckBoxStyle}">
        <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="MinHeight" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
        <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        Top-level (non-themed) keyed Style consumed by TableViewRow::RebuildCells
        via LookupElementResource. Must live OUTSIDE ThemeDictionaries so the
        ancestor-walk → Application.Resources fallback can find it. (Keys defined
        only inside ThemeDictionaries are dispatched per-theme; keys defined here
        at top level are visible at the Application.Resources scope after the
        build pipeline aggregates this file into the dll's themeresources.xaml.)

        The Setter VALUE uses {ThemeResource}, so each cell wrapper's BorderBrush
        is re-resolved against its ActualTheme — same path as the horizontal-line
        Setter in the TableViewRow Style at TableView.xaml:260, which always
        rendered correctly per-theme.

        Why a keyed Style and not a programmatic LookupElementResource brush in
        C++: the programmatic path resolves against Application.RequestedTheme
        (Default = follow-OS = Light on most boxes), so setting RequestedTheme=Dark
        on the root XAML produced wrong-theme vertical gridlines (Light brush over
        Dark background, RGB(33) on bg 39 = invisible).

        BorderThickness is intentionally NOT in this Style — RefreshGridLines sets
        a local BorderThickness=0,0,1,0 when vertical lines are wanted and
        ClearValues it otherwise. Keeping thickness out of the Style lets consumers
        override the BorderBrush via implicit-by-key styling without inheriting an
        opinionated thickness.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="TableViewCellWrapperStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource TabularSurfaceGridLineBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        group-header-brush-fix (Option-F) — keyed Style consumed by
        TableViewRow::RenderGroupHeader. Same rationale as
        TableViewCellWrapperStyle above: programmatic LookupElementResource of
        a brush resolves once at call time against the element's then-current
        theme context and returns a concrete SolidColorBrush instance that
        does NOT swap on a subsequent theme change. Wrapping the Background
        assignment in a Setter with {ThemeResource ...} delegates the value to
        the XAML markup-extension pipeline, which subscribes to theme
        transitions and re-resolves on Light↔Dark switches — eliminating the
        "group header keeps the old-theme background after switch" symptom.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="TableViewGroupHeaderBorderStyle" TargetType="Border">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource TabularSurfaceGroupHeaderBackgroundBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
        TableViewDefaultBandingStyle — opt-in keyed Style that turns on the
        canonical Excel-zebra row banding without consumers having to wire up
        four DPs (RowBackground / AlternatingRowBackground / RowForeground /
        AlternatingRowForeground) themselves.

        Why a keyed Style and not a programmatic Application.Current.Resources
        lookup in code-behind: same root cause as TableViewCellWrapperStyle /
        TableViewGroupHeaderBorderStyle above — Application.Resources lookup of
        a brush resolves against Application.RequestedTheme (immutable post
        startup), so consumers that switch theme via FrameworkElement.RequestedTheme
        on the root see wrong-theme brushes locked in. Wrapping each value in a
        Setter with {ThemeResource ...} delegates resolution to the per-element
        markup-extension pipeline, which subscribes to theme transitions and
        re-resolves on Light↔Dark switches — eliminating "table rows stay light
        after the chrome flips to dark" symptoms.

        Consumer one-liner:
            <controls:TableView Style="{ThemeResource TableViewDefaultBandingStyle}" />
        or in code-behind:
            tableView.Style = (Style)Application.Current.Resources["TableViewDefaultBandingStyle"];

        Per-row brushes default to null in the TableView DP metadata (banding
        remains strictly opt-in for hosts that want clean rendering); applying
        this Style flips all four on with the theme-tracked palette.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="TableViewDefaultBandingStyle" TargetType="controls:TableView">
        <Setter Property="RowBackground" Value="{ThemeResource TableViewDefaultRowBackgroundBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="AlternatingRowBackground" Value="{ThemeResource TableViewDefaultAlternatingRowBackgroundBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="RowForeground" Value="{ThemeResource TableViewDefaultRowForegroundBrush}" />
        <Setter Property="AlternatingRowForeground" Value="{ThemeResource TableViewDefaultRowForegroundBrush}" />
    </Style>
</ResourceDictionary>
